														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4689277">
									

<p>The idea of an anomaly is inconsistent here. All anomalies are things that the universe says is true, even thought it shouldn't &#8212; the universe says that a particular statue can move, or there's a number between █ and █, or that SCP-055 is a self-keeping secret. The whole <em>point</em> of the Foundation is that these things exist despite contradicting what we formerly presumed to be true.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Such things are not anomalous, as they are a fact of baseline reality; they always have been, and always will be, true</p>
</blockquote>
<p>And this isn't what &quot;anomalous&quot; means. &quot;Anomalous&quot; means that it's something that contradicts what we think is true, but continues to exist regardless. I really, <em>really</em> don't like the redefinition of bureaucratohazard &#8212; an anomalous facet of law &#8212; to encompass pretty much everything anomalous, particularly when you go out of your way to wax philosophical about how actually, no, this one is special.</p>

							</div>

								
					
