														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4689288">
									

<p>But in that description, you've basically stated the core flaw &#8212; it's <span style="text-decoration: underline;">often in ways that don't mesh correctly</span>. If it was a perfect, undetectable restructuring of core concepts, I could appreciate that being given its own terminology; hell, at that point I'd agree that 'anomaly' is maybe an invalid term. But a <em>flawed</em> version of the same system, to me at least, doesn't seem to be different to any other given anomaly. If it's possible to detect this bureaucratohazard, it means <em>by definition</em> that there must be an alternative normality with which to compare it, which means that it can still be classified as abnormal, and is therefore just an anomaly with a fancy new name. If there wasn't an alternative normality then I could see your point, but that's clearly not what's happening here.</p>
<p>A little further down in that same memo is the line</p>
<blockquote>
<p>even if anomalies are irrational, they are <em>factual</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230;which should help to outline my problem here. A bureaucratohazard is <em>still an anomaly</em>. And so are any other anomalies which conflate the real with the abstract, and the physical with the metaphysical.</p>

							</div>

								
					
